WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.942 --> 00:00:05.455
I am really close with my

00:00:05.455 --> 00:00:07.809
grandfather, he just turned 85.

00:00:07.809 --> 00:00:08.976
So when I was thinking about

00:00:08.976 --> 00:00:10.346
the type of company that I wanted

00:00:10.346 --> 00:00:11.765
to build, and the type of person I

00:00:11.765 --> 00:00:12.678
wanted to build for,

00:00:12.678 --> 00:00:13.814
I think about my grandpa.

00:00:13.814 --> 00:00:15.816
[MUSIC]

00:00:15.816 --> 00:00:17.633
I grew up really close with my

00:00:17.633 --> 00:00:19.754
grandfather from the time that he

00:00:19.754 --> 00:00:21.116
was in his 90s, and

00:00:21.116 --> 00:00:22.933
I saw him really struggled to

00:00:22.933 --> 00:00:24.769
get the care that he needed.

00:00:24.769 --> 00:00:26.694
That motivated me to dive deep and

00:00:26.694 --> 00:00:28.274
figure out, how can I start

00:00:28.274 --> 00:00:30.282
a company that makes this better.

00:00:30.282 --> 00:00:32.622
[MUSIC]

00:00:32.622 --> 00:00:33.239
Sarah and

00:00:33.239 --> 00:00:35.477
I met at an admitted students event

00:00:35.477 --> 00:00:37.722
before Stanford events started.

00:00:37.722 --> 00:00:39.257
&gt;&gt; When we were applying to the GSB

00:00:39.257 --> 00:00:41.088
independently, each of us wrote our

00:00:41.088 --> 00:00:42.388
essays about why we wanted

00:00:42.388 --> 00:00:44.114
to start a company in healthcare.

00:00:44.114 --> 00:00:46.099
&gt;&gt; We were at a happy hour and

00:00:46.099 --> 00:00:48.092
Sarah overheard me say,

00:00:48.092 --> 00:00:49.772
&gt;&gt; I'm coming to Stanford because I

00:00:49.772 --> 00:00:51.635
want to start a healthcare company.

00:00:51.635 --> 00:00:54.174
And I think I ran across the room,

00:00:54.174 --> 00:00:54.815
[LAUGH].

00:00:54.815 --> 00:00:56.216
We knew pretty quickly that we

00:00:56.216 --> 00:00:57.960
wanted to start a company together.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:00:59.705
It's very clear to us that we

00:00:59.705 --> 00:01:01.816
wanted to build an impact oriented

00:01:01.816 --> 00:01:04.074
company that made healthcare better

00:01:04.074 --> 00:01:05.252
for older adults.

00:01:05.252 --> 00:01:10.228
[MUSIC]

00:01:10.228 --> 00:01:11.110
At FairStreet,

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:12.937
we help seniors enroll in the right

00:01:12.937 --> 00:01:14.575
health insurance plan, and we

00:01:14.575 --> 00:01:16.402
do that by empowering independent

00:01:16.402 --> 00:01:18.103
insurance agents with the tools

00:01:18.103 --> 00:01:19.930
they need to run their business and

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:21.069
serve more seniors.

00:01:21.069 --> 00:01:22.936
&gt;&gt; What we realized after doing

00:01:22.936 --> 00:01:25.037
a couple 100 interviews with

00:01:25.037 --> 00:01:27.215
seniors all across the country,

00:01:27.215 --> 00:01:29.470
is that the universal pain point is

00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:31.649
how to navigate the overwhelming

00:01:31.649 --> 00:01:33.851
nature of Medicare enrollment.

00:01:33.851 --> 00:01:35.363
From there we looked at, well,

00:01:35.363 --> 00:01:36.875
who had a really good experience

00:01:36.875 --> 00:01:38.219
enrolling in Medicare, and

00:01:38.219 --> 00:01:38.899
who didn't.

00:01:38.899 --> 00:01:40.209
And the people who had the best

00:01:40.209 --> 00:01:41.522
experiences were the ones who

00:01:41.522 --> 00:01:42.573
worked with these local

00:01:42.573 --> 00:01:43.585
independent agents.

00:01:43.585 --> 00:01:44.981
&gt;&gt; So what we did is we went out

00:01:44.981 --> 00:01:46.631
and we interviewed hundreds of

00:01:46.631 --> 00:01:48.598
independent insurance agents, and

00:01:48.598 --> 00:01:50.438
figured out that they lack any kind

00:01:50.438 --> 00:01:52.349
of software to run their business.

00:01:52.349 --> 00:01:53.669
&gt;&gt; And so that's exactly where we

00:01:53.669 --> 00:01:54.589
saw an opportunity.

00:01:54.589 --> 00:01:56.474
&gt;&gt; A software that we built is

00:01:56.474 --> 00:01:57.502
a solution for

00:01:57.502 --> 00:02:00.161
these amazing independent agents to

00:02:00.161 --> 00:02:02.401
be able to run their business.

00:02:02.401 --> 00:02:04.067
&gt;&gt; The best compliment we've ever

00:02:04.067 --> 00:02:05.731
gotten from a senior is saying,

00:02:05.731 --> 00:02:06.539
that was easy.

00:02:06.539 --> 00:02:08.295
I feel confident I know how to use

00:02:08.295 --> 00:02:09.343
my Medicare plan.

00:02:09.343 --> 00:02:12.229
I feel like I'm set up for

00:02:12.229 --> 00:02:14.984
the right healthcare.

00:02:16.266 --> 00:02:17.713
So Stanford teaches us to dream

00:02:17.713 --> 00:02:19.327
really big, and changing lives,

00:02:19.327 --> 00:02:20.894
organizations, and the world.

00:02:20.894 --> 00:02:22.082
I think a lot about our

00:02:22.082 --> 00:02:24.005
role in changing organizations.

00:02:24.005 --> 00:02:25.575
A big motivator for me personally

00:02:25.575 --> 00:02:27.201
in starting a company it's to form

00:02:27.201 --> 00:02:28.772
and create an organization that I

00:02:28.772 --> 00:02:30.398
would have always wanted to work at

00:02:30.398 --> 00:02:31.197
as an employee.

00:02:31.197 --> 00:02:32.751
And as two female co-founders

00:02:32.751 --> 00:02:34.605
ourselves, it's something that we

00:02:34.605 --> 00:02:35.977
think a lot about is how do we

00:02:35.977 --> 00:02:37.413
create an inclusive culture

00:02:37.413 --> 00:02:38.609
an equitable culture,

00:02:38.609 --> 00:02:40.342
a culture where everyone can get to

00:02:40.342 --> 00:02:41.774
show up and do the best work of

00:02:41.774 --> 00:02:43.457
their life &gt;&gt; Before coming

00:02:43.457 --> 00:02:46.063
to the GSB I thought that business

00:02:46.063 --> 00:02:48.773
was very strict and very factual.

00:02:48.773 --> 00:02:51.286
And at the GSB you learn about how

00:02:51.286 --> 00:02:54.078
much emotion and feelings play into

00:02:54.078 --> 00:02:56.230
what goes on in a business.

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:58.120
My grandfather would definitely

00:02:58.120 --> 00:02:59.542
be proud of FairStreet.

00:02:59.542 --> 00:03:01.188
And I think that the thing

00:03:01.188 --> 00:03:03.384
you would hope for the most is that

00:03:03.384 --> 00:03:05.110
other people have a better

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:07.554
healthcare experience than he did.

00:03:07.554 --> 00:03:10.186
Our whole family would have liked

00:03:10.186 --> 00:03:12.348
to spend the last years of my

00:03:12.348 --> 00:03:14.980
grandfather's life together as

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:17.518
a family, and not trying to wade

00:03:17.518 --> 00:03:19.586
through medical bills and

00:03:19.586 --> 00:03:21.285
medical decisions.

00:03:21.285 --> 00:03:22.614
If he could see other families

00:03:22.614 --> 00:03:24.211
having that better experience I'm

00:03:24.211 --> 00:03:25.497
sure he would be very proud.

00:03:25.497 --> 00:03:28.445
[MUSIC]

00:03:28.445 --> 00:03:29.923
&gt;&gt; For our ambition to change the

00:03:29.923 --> 00:03:31.884
world, we're just getting started.

00:03:31.884 --> 00:03:33.448
Our hope is that if we're

00:03:33.448 --> 00:03:35.163
successful, every single

00:03:35.163 --> 00:03:37.249
person turning 65 does not have

00:03:37.249 --> 00:03:39.421
to worry about their healthcare.

00:03:39.421 --> 00:03:40.960
That they're taken care of by

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:42.374
FairStreet and by one of the

00:03:42.374 --> 00:03:44.158
amazing agents that we work with,

00:03:44.158 --> 00:03:45.819
so that they can have healthcare

00:03:45.819 --> 00:03:47.248
for the rest of their life.

00:03:47.248 --> 00:03:48.549
My name is Sarah Jacobson,

00:03:48.549 --> 00:03:49.738
I'm the Co-Founder and

00:03:49.738 --> 00:03:50.706
CEO of FairStreet.

00:03:50.706 --> 00:03:52.575
&gt;&gt; My name is Tori Seidenstein, and

00:03:52.575 --> 00:03:53.978
I'm the Co-Founder and

00:03:53.978 --> 00:03:55.123
